    "problem_statement": "# HTML table parsing issue with PrettyTable\n\nI've found an issue with the HTML table parsing functionality in PrettyTable. When trying to convert an HTML table back to a PrettyTable object, it's not working correctly.\n\n## Description\n\nWhen using the `from_html()` or `from_html_one()` functions to parse an HTML table that was previously generated with `get_html_string()`, the resulting PrettyTable object doesn't match the original table.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom prettytable import PrettyTable, from_html, from_html_one\n\n# Create a table\ntable = PrettyTable()\ntable.field_names = [\"City name\", \"Area\", \"Population\", \"Annual Rainfall\"]\ntable.add_row([\"Adelaide\", 1295, 1158259, 600.5])\ntable.add_row([\"Brisbane\", 5905, 1857594, 1146.4])\ntable.add_row([\"Darwin\", 112, 120900, 1714.7])\n\n# Convert to HTML\nhtml_string = table.get_html_string()\n\n# Try to convert back\nnew_table = from_html(html_string)[0]  # This doesn't match the original\n# OR\nnew_table = from_html_one(html_string)  # This also doesn't match the original\n\n# Verify\nprint(new_table.get_string() == table.get_string())  # Returns False\n```\n\nThe issue seems to be related to how the HTML parser is handling table headers. When examining the HTML parsing code, I noticed that it's looking for \"th\" tags to identify headers, but something is going wrong in the process."
  },
